{align:justify|Mr. and Mrs. Hua were merchants living in Malaysia, with a son named Vu Hung. Hung would not study diligently but loved bad company, roaming and idling outside day after day.}
{align:justify|Mr. Hua could only send his son to England to study, hoping that far from his bad friends he might yet turn out well.}
{align:justify|In England, Hung remained unchanged, roaming everywhere day after day, spending money on pleasure.}
{align:justify|Until Mr. Hua's business failed and money ran short, forcing him to call his son home from his studies. But Hung was already addicted to drugs: far from helping his father's enterprise, he sold off the house and all of the old man's remaining property. Mr. Hua sickened with grief, and before his dying breath he repented and confessed a truth no one had ever known:}
{align:justify|Long ago, he had been secretary to a rich merchant — but he coveted his employer's wife. Once, while his master was home visiting his native village, he laid a trap, staging a car accident that killed his master horribly. And so all his master's possessions, fortune and wife alike, fell into his hands.}
{align:justify|Every wish fulfilled, Mr. Hua and the merchant's widow fled far away to build their lovebirds' nest elsewhere.}
{align:justify|A year later they had a son — the precious Vu Hung himself.}
{align:justify|And in the end, after squandering his father's money, Hung sold off the whole family estate, until even after his father died he could not afford a coffin. His mother too died of tumors. The Hua line fell utterly into "house ruined, lives lost."}
